    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashta.jpg" alt="Kashta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_-_Situla_Bearing_the_names_of_Kashta_and_Amenirdis_-_Walters_543077.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous (Egypt)Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushite Empire, also known as the Kingdom of Kush, arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, heir to the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture that flourished from around 2500 BCE until its conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom Egypt</a> around 1500 BCE. A new Kushite state took shape at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, near the holy mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, after Egyptian control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> collapsed with the end of the New Kingdom around 1070 BCE and local elites reasserted independence. Imperial expansion is closely associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> (also known as Piankhi), who around 728 BCE defeated the Delta coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tefnakht">Tefnakht of Sais</a> and recorded his triumph on a great victory stela at Jebel Barkal. Under Piye's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a>, Kushite rule was consolidated as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</a> of Egypt, uniting Nubia and Egypt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Fourth Cataract</a>. The empire's rise rested on control of gold resources and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> trade routes, and on its adoption of Egyptian political and religious traditions, which allowed Kushite kings to present themselves as restorers of order in a fragmented Nile Valley.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/meroe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroe.jpg" alt="Meroë" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NubianMeroePyramids30sep2005(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wufei07, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushite economy rested on Nile-based agriculture and access to valuable natural resources, particularly gold. The floodplains supported barley, wheat and, later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, sustaining rural communities and towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding remained central to wealth and status. Society was stratified, with a ruling elite combining indigenous Nubian traditions and Egyptian cultural influence, alongside priests, artisans, farmers and labourers. Trade played a central role, linking the African interior with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world: gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, exotic animals and skins were exchanged for luxury and manufactured goods. The shift of the royal centre to Meroë, completed by around 300 BCE, marked an economic transformation, as the region, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> grazing and rain-fed farming beyond the river, became a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> fuelled by abundant wood for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>; its slag heaps remain visible today. Tribute from subordinate regions reinforced state revenues, supporting long-term economic and political stability.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/piye"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piye.jpg" alt="Piye" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_Piye_Mariette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Mariette (1821-1881), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The military strength of the Kushite Empire was integral to its expansion and defence, combining indigenous Nubian martial traditions with Egyptian practice. The army relied heavily on infantry and skilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, long the hallmark of Nubian warfare in a land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called Ta-Seti, the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-the-bow">Land of the Bow</a>”. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> and his successors Kushite forces campaigned through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, overcoming fragmented local rulers and asserting control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a>. Chariotry and fine horses, which Piye prized and which Assyrian records later sought out, were valued elements of Kushite power. In the seventh century BCE, however, the empire faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a> in 671 BCE, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>'s attempts to recover Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s army sacked Thebes in 663 BCE, ending the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> in Egypt and forcing a retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Despite this setback, the Kushite army defended its core territories for centuries; in the 20s BCE it raided Roman Egypt, provoking a punitive expedition under the prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-petronius-prefect-of-egypt">Petronius</a> that ended in a negotiated peace favourable to Kush.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharaoh.jpg" alt="Pharaoh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Double_crown.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeff Dahl, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Governance in the Kushite Empire was characterised by a centralised monarchy that drew heavily on Egyptian models while retaining indigenous elements. The king, styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</a>, exercised supreme political and religious authority, supported by governors, priests and officials. The cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, played a central role in legitimising royal power, and kings were chosen from among eligible royal kinsmen with the god's oracular approval. Law was not codified in the manner of some later states but rooted in custom and religious principle, with priests and officials adjudicating disputes. The move of the royal centre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> brought gradual diversification of government, with greater emphasis on regional administration. Royal women held exceptional influence: queen mothers played a recognised role in succession, and several queens, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandake">kandakes</a>, ruled in their own right, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanirenas">Amanirenas</a>, who confronted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanishakheto">Amanishakheto</a>. This blend of central authority and regional flexibility enabled the state to endure across a geographically diverse territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">René Hourdry, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kushite Empire exerted a profound influence on both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> and the wider African interior, acting as a cultural and economic bridge between regions. Its rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</a> fostered a revival of traditional Egyptian religious and artistic forms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>'s building works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> rank among the finest of the age. Kushite trade networks reached deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, spreading goods, technologies and cultural forms across vast distances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> became a leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> and craft. The Kushites revived pyramid building for their royal tombs, constructing more pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-kurru">El-Kurru</a>, Nuri and Meroë than were built in Egypt itself, and developed their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroitic-script">Meroitic script</a>, whose signs can be read but whose language is still only partly understood, reflecting growing cultural independence from Egypt. Interaction with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> placed Kush within a wider international system. Its legacy persisted in regional traditions, political institutions and economic patterns long after its political decline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ezana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezana.jpg" alt="Ezana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ezana">Ezana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AXUM._Ezanas._Circa_330-360.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Kushite Empire was a gradual process shaped by internal transformation and external pressure. After the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> in the seventh century BCE, power was concentrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, and after an Egyptian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> around 593 BCE the political centre moved progressively southward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Over the following centuries environmental strain, possibly including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> linked to iron smelting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a>, may have undermined the agricultural base of the state. Trade routes also changed, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> commerce increasingly bypassed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> corridor, reducing Kush's strategic importance. Externally, the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a> posed a growing threat. Around 350 CE an inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a> records the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-invasion-of-kush">Aksumite invasion of Kush</a>, a campaign along the Nile against the Noba and Kasu, by which time Meroë appears already to have been in decline. Internal fragmentation and weakening central authority accelerated the collapse, and by the mid-fourth century CE Kush had ceased to exist as a unified political entity, leaving behind a rich archaeological and cultural legacy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kushite Empire stands as the great Nubian civilisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</a>, developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroitic-script">Meroitic script</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>'s iron industry, and linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world for centuries.</em></p>

